WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:06.240
Originally introduced in 1966 in one of the
most memorable moments in Spider-Man history,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:12.040
Mary Jane has had a career on the page that's
almost as complicated as her radioactive boyfriend's.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.260
From her comic book origins to her on-screen
adaptations, here's the untold truth behind

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.260
Mary Jane Watson.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.900
While she wouldn't make her full appearance
until December 1966, Mary Jane was first mentioned

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.300
more than two years earlier.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.580
It was actually a running gag in the comic,
with Aunt May constantly trying to wrangle

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:36.260
Peter into a blind date with the niece of
her next-door neighbor, and Peter making every

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.520
excuse that he could to duck out and avoid
actually meeting her.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.620
The joke, of course, was that Peter assumed
anyone who needed their aged aunt to set them

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.310
up on a blind date was, to put it charitably,
probably not the most desirable person for

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:50.960
a relationship.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.900
For the record, Peter was also someone who
needed his elderly aunt to set him up on dates.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:03.160
Finally, after 27 issues of MJ looming ominously
like a go-go dancing Sword of Damocles, came

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:08.480
Amazing Spider-Man #42, from Stan Lee and
John Romita Sr.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.200
After a relatively forgettable fight between
Spider-Man and hero astronaut and future werewolf

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.380
space god John Jameson, Mary Jane Watson made
her first full appearance, and we finally

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.200
got the punchline.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.820
It turned out that MJ was actually hot.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.460
Putting aside the slightly sketchy moral lesson
about keeping an open mind because people

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:32.869
you haven't met might be physically attractive,
Mary Jane's appearance marked a huge shift

00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:34.170
in the Spider-Man dynamic.

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:41.090
For the first few years of her existence,
Mary Jane's major effect on the Amazing Spider-Man

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.320
comics was to convert a love triangle into
something that looked a little more like a

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:45.820
rhombus.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:51.460
She was pitted against Gwen Stacy, Peter Parker's
long-established dream girl, competing for

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:56.029
Peter's affection, and settling for double
dates with Peter's roommate (and future Green

00:01:56.029 --> 00:01:57.439
Goblin), Harry Osborn.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.480
Gwen, for her part, had been introduced way
back in the early days by Spider-Man's co-creators,

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.660
Stan Lee and Steve Ditko.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.140
In her original appearances, Gwen was a very
different sort of love interest — a brilliant

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.570
science student with a short temper who often
found herself bickering with Peter and trading

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.240
sharp quips as their romantic tension grew.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.660
All that changed when Ditko left the book
and Romita stepped in as Ditko's replacement

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.600
and arguably became the definitive artist
of the era.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:32.280
The main change came from bringing his experience
from doing romance comics at DC, and he transformed

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.920
Peter from an awkward, angular teenager to
a handsome college student.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.480
Romita also applied the same style to the
rest of the cast.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:44.680
Gwen started spending a lot of time in go-go
boots and minidresses, and MJ, earned her

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.720
reputation as the consummate party girl ... right
up until the Green Goblin changed Spidey's

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:50.900
supporting cast forever.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.940
Amazing Spider-Man #121 will live in infamy
for being, as the title of the issue revealed

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.510
on its very last page, "The Night Gwen Stacy
Died."

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:06.740
If you're even passingly familiar with Spider-Man's
history, you know how this one goes: After

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:12.200
discovering Spidey's secret identity, the
Green Goblin kidnaps Gwen and winds up tossing

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.220
her off of the George Washington Bridge.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:19.320
And when Spider-Man snags her with a web-line
in an attempt to save her life, he accidentally

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.180
snaps her neck.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:31.360
It was a huge shift for the Spider-Man saga,
and it remains one of the most important deaths

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.450
in Marvel Comics history (and along with Uncle
Ben's, it's one of the few that's been carved

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.140
into stone and never undone).

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.610
What's often overlooked, though, is that the
issue that followed represented a change that

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.140
was almost as big, and not just because the
Goblin himself "died."

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.120
After Gwen's death, Mary Jane shows up at
Peter's apartment, and in his grief, Peter

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:58.280
snaps at her, offering up some extremely harsh
comments about MJ's seemingly shallow personality.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.360
When he tells her to leave, though, Mary Jane
chooses to stay and help him through the tragedy.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.400
It was the first sign readers got that MJ
was a little deeper than we'd been led to

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:12.290
believe, and it also hinted at a major reveal
that wouldn't hit the page for another 16

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:12.780
years.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:20.120
In 1989, Marvel published Amazing Spider-Man:
Parallel Lives, an oversized graphic novel

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.600
focusing on Peter and Mary Jane as they grow
up, and the ways their lives intersect.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:30.240
Over the course of flashbacks, the story reveals
that Mary Jane had taken a keen interest in

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.540
Spider-Man from the very beginning.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:36.480
Her fascination only increased on the night
that Ben Parker was shot, when she looked

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.360
out the window of her aunt's house and saw
Spider-Man climbing out of her neighbor's

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:40.860
window.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.160
She immediately realized that Peter Parker
was Spider-Man, and comic book fans suddenly

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.620
realized that MJ had always known about his
secret identity.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:54.020
Instead of being the blind date that Aunt
May and Anna Watson had worked so hard to

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:58.800
set up, Mary Jane had a new context as someone
who'd pursued Peter herself.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.560
It wasn't because of a misguided obsession
with Spidey, or because she had a thing for

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.060
superheroes.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:08.460
Instead, it was because she'd found a parallel
— get it? — between the way he wore a

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:13.330
mask and kept his heroic identity separate
from the tragedies of his personal life and

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:18.430
the way that she had always affected that
flighty, shallow persona to mask the trauma

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:19.600
of her abusive upbringing.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.030
More than anything else, the idea that she'd
kept Peter's identity a secret for years without

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:29.200
him even knowing it put her in a completely
different class than any other love interest

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.250
in Spider-Man history.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:35.870
In a lot of ways, the Spider-Man saga is about
watching Peter Parker grow up.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:39.840
He starts his superheroic career as a high
school student who lets his bitterness at

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.200
being a social outcast cause him to give into
selfishness.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.060
"You coulda taken that guy apart and now he’s
gonna get away with my money.”

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.040
“I missed the part where that’s my problem."

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.000
He then has to deal with the consequences
of those actions.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:58.710
Over the next few years, readers saw him stand
up to a bully, get a job, and deal with all

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.660
the other milestones of adulthood that most
folks go through, even if they don't fight

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.540
Doctor Octopus and the Lizard in between.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.780
In 1987, Peter and Mary Jane reached the next
big life milestone together when they were

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.600
married on the steps of New York City Hall
in Amazing Spider-Man Annual #21.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.880
The thing is, it almost didn't happen, at
least in the context of the story.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:24.040
Even once they realized they wanted to be
married, and even after Peter proposed twice,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.260
they both still had a lot of doubts.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.220
That was what made Amazing Spider-Man Annual
#21 such an interesting comic.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.330
Plenty of superhero weddings feature villains
showing up, but this story was focused on

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:40.120
Peter trying to decide if he should break
things off, with MJ herself being tempted

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.560
to run away with a famous actor.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.800
In the end, of course, the two got hitched.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:48.699
As for the real-world reason behind the wedding,
Stan Lee claims credit for coming up with

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:54.240
it as a publicity stunt, and also performed
a live-action version of it at Shea Stadium.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.650
The wedding also happened in the Spider-Man
newspaper strip that Lee was writing, with

00:06:58.650 --> 00:06:59.140
an extra detail.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:03.900
That version of the marriage was dedicated
to fashion designer Willi Smith, who created

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.720
Mary Jane's wedding dress and appeared in
the Annual.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.930
Sadly, he would die of AIDS-related illness
at the age of 39, shortly before the issue

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:12.500
was published.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.260
For years, it was rumored that higher-ups
at Marvel Comics wanted to get rid of Mary

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.669
Jane and Peter's marriage due to worries about
it making him seem old.

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:25.740
The problem was that they didn't want to be
stuck with a Spider-Man who'd been widowed

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.160
or divorced, which would make him seem even
older than the marriage did.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:33.880
"The Wife and I split up, but I handled it like a champ!"

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:40.360
It was a tough problem, but eventually, they
came up with a simple, elegant solution: Just

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.240
have somebody make a deal with the devil.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:43.480
Like, literally.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:44.680
The literal devil.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.180
No, really!

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.580
It happened in the aftermath of Civil War,
a crossover that, among other things, had

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:56.199
Spider-Man publicly reveal his secret identity,
which immediately led to Aunt May getting

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:57.280
shot by a hitman.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:02.820
In order to save Aunt May and put the "secret"
back into Peter's secret identity, Peter and

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:08.540
MJ essentially sold their marriage to Mephisto,
the Marvel Universe's most stalwart stand-in

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.550
for Satan, in a story called "One More Day."

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:15.240
The second weirdest thing about this — other
than the part where Spider-Man and his wife

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.340
were making Faustian bargains — was that
it didn't really disrupt continuity all that

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:19.840
much.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.360
The official company line was that every Spider-Man
story that had taken place during the married

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.600
years had still happened.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.590
The only thing that had been retroactively
changed was the wedding itself.

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.610
The post-"One More Day" idea was that all
of the couples' previous doubts had actually

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:39.280
gotten to them, and while they'd kept on living
together, they just never tied the knot.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:40.280
Actually, no.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:44.500
The real weirdest thing was that, just as
it had with the wedding, the newspaper strip

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:49.110
attempted to do the same, but those readers
hate change even more than superhero comic

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.850
readers do, and the reaction was so bad that
the new storyline only lasted a short time

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.080
before it was all re-retconned into just being
a weird dream.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:01.100
In the newspapers, Spider-Man and MJ are still
happily married.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:05.960
If you're less than four degrees of separation
away from Peter Parker, the odds are pretty

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:10.600
good that you've been a superhero, a supervillain,
or both at some point in your life.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:16.040
His college roommate became the Green Goblin,
and his science teacher was the Jackal.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:21.370
A coworker became Venom, two other coworkers
were both the Hobgoblin at various times,

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:24.320
and one of them was also another Green Goblin.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.760
His boss' son was a space werewolf.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:33.560
Even Aunt May was once taken into space and
reborn as Golden Oldie, Herald of Galactus.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:34.780
The one big exception?

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:35.880
Mary Jane Watson.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:41.950
Despite being in close proximity to Spider-Man
for over 50 years of real-time comics, MJ

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:43.760
never got any superpowers of her own.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:49.320
The only big exception came during the events
of the "Spider-Island" crossover, but considering

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:53.910
that everyone in Manhattan briefly got Spider-Man
powers during that story, that doesn't really

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:54.400
count.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:10:00.200
There was, however, an issue of Marvel Team-Up
back in the late '70s where MJ was briefly

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:05.560
possessed by the spirit of Red Sonja, who
was brought forward in time to fight an evil

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:06.060
wizard.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.860
Really, though, that's just a pretty normal
Tuesday for the Marvel Universe.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.500
Given her prominence in the comics, it's no
surprise that out of the four big-screen versions

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.990
of the Spider-Man story that have hit the
big screen since 2002, Mary Jane Watson has

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:21.820
appeared in three of them ... sort of.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:27.720
She's completely absent in the two Amazing
Spider-Man movies from 2012 and 2014, with

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.540
Emma Stone as Gwen Stacy serving as the love
interest in those films.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:36.480
In the Spider-Man trilogy directed by Sam
Raimi, MJ is one of the most prominent characters.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:41.580
Kirsten Dunst co-starred in all three films,
and her version of Mary Jane provided most

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.460
of the non-superhero drama.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:48.180
She's there from Peter's sad decision to keep
his identity secret in the first movie all

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.339
the way to their relationship difficulties
in the third film — made worse, of course,

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:57.760
by an alien symbiote that drove Peter to stage
one of cinema's most mean-spirited dance numbers.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.400
Unfortunately, all that drama led to some
criticism of the character for being needlessly

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.200
harsh towards her city-saving boyfriend.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.400
MJ has also appeared in the MCU Spider-Man
films, in a way.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:14.920
Peter's main love interest in those, particularly
in Far From Home, is Michelle, played by Zendaya.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.260
"Why are you here, you don’t even HAVE detention.”

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:18.260
“Oh, I know.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.280
I just like coming here to sketch people in
crisis.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.980
Heh heh, that's you."

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:31.280
It's not until the end of Spider-Man: Homecoming
that viewers learn her full name is Michelle

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:33.960
Jones, and that her friends call her MJ.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:38.610
This MJ is very different from what we've
seen in the comics, but those initials are

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.040
definitely not a coincidence.

